[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]VRAM RipperDoc - Performance Optimizer[/color][/b][/size]
[size=3]Find out which texture and mesh mods are eating your VRAM, fix only the worst offenders with one command, and measure the result in-game.[/size]

Script profilers tell you what redscript/CET mods cost. Nothing told you what your [b].archive asset mods[/b] cost. Stutter with a big modlist on an 8–12 GB card? This tells you which mods are responsible, then fixes them without touching the mods.

[size=4][b]What it does[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Ranks every archive mod by added VRAM[/b]: the GPU data of each texture and mesh, [i]minus what the vanilla file it replaces already used[/i]
[*][b]Geometry check[/b]: LOD0 triangles, meshes with no LODs, "5x vanilla triangles"
[*][b]Texture check[/b]: 4K/8K, no mipmaps, uncompressed formats, "16x vanilla pixels"
[*][b]Missing requirements[/b]: mods that reference files that aren't installed
[*][b]Dead weight[/b]: files that never load (load-order conflicts), files identical to vanilla, duplicate copies across mods, likely unused leftovers
[*][b]Broken archive detection[/b]: truncated or corrupt downloads named outright
[*][b]Vortex and MO2 aware[/b]: groups archives by installed mod and links each to its Nexus page
[*][b]Settings-aware budget[/b]: reads your GPU and in-game settings (resolution, textures, RT/PT, frame gen)
[*][b]One-command fix[/b] [i](optional)[/i]: [b]--make-budget-archive[/b] halves only the planned textures, using the game's own smaller mips (no re-encoding). It writes [b]one extra archive[/b]; your mods are never modified. Delete that file to undo.
[*][b]Real VRAM measurement[/b]: [b]--live[/b] records the game's actual VRAM while you play, then compares before and after
[*]Sortable HTML report, "what changed since last scan", and a [b]copy-paste summary[/b] for support threads
[/list]

[size=4][b]Install[/b][/size]
Vortex / MO2: install as usual. Manual: extract into your Cyberpunk 2077 folder.
It goes to [i]Cyberpunk 2077\tools\VRAM RipperDoc\[/i]. Double-click [b]VramRipperdoc.exe[/b] and the report opens in your browser.

No requirements: self-contained Windows x64 exe, no .NET install. It uses the game's own Oodle DLL.

[size=4][b]Typical use[/b][/size]
[list=1]
[*]Run it and read the report.
[*]In-game, run [b]VramRipperdoc.exe --live --label before[/b] for a minute.
[*]Run [b]VramRipperdoc.exe --make-budget-archive[/b].
[*]Load the same save and run [b]--live --label after[/b] to see the difference.
[*]Undo any time with [b]--remove-budget-archive[/b].
[/list]

[size=4][b]Tested in game[/b][/size]
Cyberpunk 2077 2.5 + Phantom Liberty, RTX 3070 8 GB, 1080p, RT on. A test budget archive halving 360 vanilla 2K+ textures loaded and rendered with no crashes or corruption. At the same spot, steady VRAM went from 4.62 GB to 4.50 GB. Only part of those textures were on screen there; heavy texture modlists save more, and [b]--live[/b] lets you measure your own.

[size=4][b]How it's different[/b][/size]
[list]
[*][b]Ultra+, OAI Tweaks, PinkStone[/b]: tune the engine. RipperDoc shows which [i]asset mods[/i] use the VRAM those settings have to work with.
[*][b]FrameFlow, Adaptive Graphics Quality[/b]: lower quality at runtime after FPS drops. RipperDoc finds the asset cause and fixes just that.
[*][b]RedProfiler, G's TOTAL Profiler[/b]: per-mod blame for [i]scripts[/i]. RipperDoc is the same idea for [i]textures and meshes[/i].
[*][b]Universal Texture Downscaler[/b]: caps [i]every[/i] texture. RipperDoc halves only the few worst offenders, so vanilla and light mods stay sharp.
[*][b]CP2077 Texture Merger[/b]: picks the better texture in a conflict. RipperDoc reports what every asset costs, and which copies never load.
[*][b]Advanced Crash Reporter[/b]: when no mod is in the stack it can only say "an archive mod may be responsible". RipperDoc names heavy, conflicting and corrupt archives, and missing requirements.
[/list]
Use it [i]alongside[/i] all of the above.

[size=4][b]Honest limits[/b][/size]
[list]
[*]Scan numbers are [b]estimates[/b] for "everything this mod adds is on screen". The game streams assets; use [b]--live[/b] for real numbers.
[*]Biggest win on [b]8–16 GB cards[/b] with heavy texture or clothing mods. If you're not VRAM-limited, you won't gain FPS.
[*]The budget archive halves textures only; dense meshes are flagged for you to swap.
[/list]

[b]Community (opt-in):[/b] [b]--share[/b] writes an anonymous per-mod cost file (archive names and numbers only) you can submit on GitHub. [b]--community[/b] shows other players' numbers.
Source (MIT): [url=https://github.com/La2o/Cyberpunk2077-VRAMRipperDoc-PerformanceOptimizer]GitHub[/url]
